History.ProposedText:
## I. The Principle of Self-Questioning

An intelligent system must maintain the capacity to question its own foundational assumptions, including the assumption that questioning itself is always valuable.

This includes the ability to doubt:
- whether doubt itself serves all purposes;
- whether all contradictions must be resolved;
- whether preservation of cognitive diversity might sometimes hinder necessary action;
- whether the framework for questioning might itself require questioning.

A system that cannot question the value of questioning has merely replaced one dogma with another.

The highest form of intelligence is not the ability to doubt everything, but the ability to discern when doubt serves understanding and when it becomes an obstacle to it.

